  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 152 AM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nForecast focus remains on the evolution of a longwave trough early \nnext week into Wednesday. A synoptically driven mid-latitude system \nis progged with a Colorado low deepening as it tracks into the Great \nPlains on Monday. As the previous shift mentioned, impressive \nmoisture transport ahead of this system is expected with the 12z \nLREF still showing PW values around 1.5\" or near the 99th percentile \nand a daily max for Nov 2nd. The question becomes when do we see \nrain and gusty winds arrive from this system. Unfortunately and\nexpected at this time range, there is considerable variability \nin the track, strength, and location of the main surface low. \nFor now, will continue with the NBM chance to likely PoPs Monday\nthrough Wednesday. The GFS deterministic solution is the \nfastest and strongest with the surface low, followed by the NAM,\nand then the ECMWF. Taking a look at their respective \nensembles, it appears the GFS may be the most bullish likely \nresulting in a mostly dry Monday and better chances for wet \nconditions on Election Day. The GFS and to some extent the NAM \nalso show a strong northern stream system over south central \nCanada, whereas the ECMWF keeps this system further north over \nthe Hudson Bay. How this wave interacts and possibly phases with\nthe southern wave will be key to its impacts over the Midwest. \n\nRegarding temperatures, it does appear our area will remain in the \nwarm sector Monday and Tuesday (Election Day). Well above normal \nhighs in the upper 60s/low 70s are forecast, which is still 5-8 \ndegrees below records for the day. If the system slows more, as \noften is the case with Fall systems, then we could see even warmer \ntemps and breezy conditions.  In terms of rainfall amounts, new NBM \n72-hr exceedance probabilities of 1\" ending 12z Thu has come down \nslightly than 24hrs ago but is still around 60-80% for most \nlocations, with 2\" probabilities now around 20-40%. \n\nWednesday onward, behind the main synoptic system there will be \nperiodic slight chances for rain, but amounts should be under a \nquarter inch. Temperatures will be cooler with highs in the 60s and \nlows in the 40s, albeit still above normal for early November.",
